  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 151 PM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nA brief bout of upper level ridging will overspread the region on \nFriday in advance of a deepening trough over the Intermountain West. \nSoutherly flow will continue to advect in both warmth and moisture, \nkeeping highs in the upper 60s. This moisture advection will be well \nabove normal for this time of year as a plume of moisture from the \nGulf brings in PWATs in excess of 1.0 inches, with the deterministic \nECMWF having values above the climatological max at 1.2 inches! \nWhile it is possible that this value will not be achieved, the \nsignal suggests that a bountiful influx of moisture will help \nsaturate the column and support rainfall potential with this system \nSaturday.\n\nLight rainfall accumulation and an isolated rumble or two of thunder \nare expected with this system Saturday evening through Sunday. Both \nPoPs and QPF have trended upwards over the last few forecast cycles, \nwith PoPs increasing to 50-90%. Chances for rainfall greater than \n0.25 inches has also increased to 40-80%, with the greatest chances \nin eastern Iowa. Some adjustment of rain chances and amounts are \nlikely over the next several days as confidence increases in where \nthe axis of rainfall will set up.\n\nAs the system clears the area Sunday, cooler temperatures are \nexpected. This will bring us down to near-normal or even below \nnormal temps for the coming week. In addition, as flow aloft becomes \nmore zonal, a progressive pattern with multiple waves could set the \nstage for an active stretch of weather.",
